 "interpretation": "The song conveys a deep mystical experience of realizing Supreme Consciousness. In dream (svapane), the Lord arrives as pure condensed bliss (ánandaghana), going beyond the intellectual constraints of the waking mind. The presence of the Supreme encompasses the cosmos (mahákásha) and the very life vitality of the universe (mahápráńa), revealing Itself not as a rigid form, but as the harmonious rhythm of creation. The Lord's gaze is filled with grace and unconditional love (anurakti), and contact with His feet bestows spiritual liberation (vimukti). Whether a person is aware of this supreme attraction intellectually or not (jene bá ná jene), the true love of the jiiva is always directed toward the One Source alone, and that love knows no end.",
